==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|aboriginal|ly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæb.əˌɹɪd͡ʒ.n̩.ə.li/|/ˌæb.əˌɹɪd͡ʒ.ɪn.ə.li/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-aboriginally.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# From or in the earliest known times. {{defdate|First attested in the early 19th century.}}<ref>{{R:SOED5|page=6}}</ref>
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|58|text={{...}} man has lost that sense of the full awfulness of the sea which '''aboriginally''' belongs to it.}}
#* {{RQ:Darwin Variation|chapter=2|page=[https://archive.org/details/NHM_UK_L_2631475001 52-53]|text={{...}} '''aboriginally''' the horse must have inhabited countries annually covered with snow, for he long retains the instinct of scraping it away to get at the herbage beneath.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=w:Stephen Fry|title=The Ode Less Travelled: Unlocking the Poet Within|location=New York|publisher=Gotham|section=Part 2, Chapter 2, p. 145|url=https://archive.org/details/odelesstravelled00frys
|passage={{...}} music, like verse, can do ''rhythm'' but it is only poetry that can yoke words together in rhyme (sometimes, of course, and '''aboriginally''', at the service of music).}}
# In the period before [[contact]] with [[European]]s (especially with reference to peoples subjected to [[colonization]]).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1896|author=Allan Eric|title=“Buckra” Land: Two Weeks in Jamaica|location=Boston|section=Appendix|url=https://archive.org/details/buckraland00will
|passage=Xaymaca, as the island was '''aboriginally''' known, is situated in the Caribbean Sea {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|author=w:Charles F. Hockett|title=Man’s Place in Nature,|location=New York|publisher=McGraw-Hill|chapter=31|page=523|url=https://archive.org/details/mansplaceinnatur00hock
|passage={{...}} in the New World, where pots were never '''aboriginally''' shaped by turning, wheeled vehicles also were absent {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Robert L. Blakely; David S. Mathews|chapter=What Price Civilization?|editors=Miles Richardson; Malcolm C. Webb|title=The Burden of Being Civilized: An Anthropological Perspective on the Discontents of Civilization|location=Athens|publisher=University of Georgia Press|page=12|url=https://archive.org/details/burdenofbeingciv0000unse
|passage=The question is, was the disease [tuberculosis] present '''aboriginally''' in the New World, or was it introduced to Native Americans by European explorers?}}
# {{lb|en|Canada}} By [[indigenous]] [[Canadian]]s (often capitalized in this sense). {{defdate|First attested in the 1980s.}}
#* '''1987''', Kate Irving, ''What Government Does in the Western Northwest Territories,'' Yellowknife: Western Constitutional Forum,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/whatgovernmentdo0000irvi]</sup>
#*: All land subject to the claim becomes either Crown land or '''aboriginally'''-owned land.
#* '''1991''', Jim Harding, ''An Annotated Bibliography of Aboriginal-controlled Justice Programs in Canada,'' Prairie Justice Research, School of Human Justice, University of Regina, p.{{nbsp}}80,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/annotatedbibliog0000hard]</sup>
#*: It appears that lack of funding and control led to the demise of this program, but that with further refinement the idea has merit especially within an '''Aboriginally'''-controlled justice system.
#* '''2002''', Bradford W. Morse and Robert K. Groves, “Métis and Non-status Indians and Section 91(24) of the ''Constitution Act, 1867'' in Paul L.A.H. Chartrand (ed.), ''Who Are Canada’s Aboriginal Peoples?'' Saskatoon: Purich Publishing, pp.{{nbsp}}209-210,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/whoarecanadasabo0000unse]</sup>
#*: These areas {{...}} relate to the identity of '''Aboriginally''' predominant communities.
# To the [[utmost]] [[degree]] (modifying an adjective).
#: {{syn|en|absolutely|thoroughly|utterly}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=w:Greville MacDonald|title=The Sanity of {{w|William Blake}}|location=London|publisher=George Allen and Unwin|page=24|url=https://archive.org/details/sanityofwilliamb00macduoft
|passage=Though his rage against iniquity is '''aboriginally''' simple and childlike, and is certainly not always level-headed, it is never divorced from reason {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1931|author=w:G. K. Chesterton|chapter=Dickens at Christmas|editor=Marie Smith|title=The Spirit of Christmas: Stories, Poems, Essays|location=New York|publisher=Dodd, Mead|year_published=1985|page=77|url=https://archive.org/details/spiritofchristma00ches
|passage=There is something '''aboriginally''' absurd in the idea of the old gentleman staring wild-eyed at his own legs; and half recalling something familiar about them; as if he were revisiting the landscape of his youth.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1978|author=w:Iris Murdoch|title=w:The Sea, the Sea|url=https://archive.org/details/seas00murd|chapter=3|pages=181–182|publisher=Chatto & Windus|location=London
|passage=Dried apricots eaten with cake should be soaked and simmered first, eaten with cheese they should be '''aboriginally''' dry.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Bella Bathurst|title=The Wreckers|location=Boston|publisher=Houghton Mifflin|chapter=5|page=152|url=https://archive.org/details/wreckersstoryofk00bath
|passage={{...}} those travellers who did make the trip [to the {{w|Outer Hebrides|Western Isles}}] returned with stories which made Scotland and the Scots sound as '''aboriginally''' exotic as shark-eating Eskimos or man-eating pygmies.}}

